Burning Mouth Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Bilateral burning sensation of the tongue, Dysgeusia (bitter/metallic taste), Relief of symptoms while eating, Visually normal oral mucosa

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-004
Reason for Visit: Severe, intractable oral pain and acute distress causing absolute inability to sleep and severe dehydration anxiety.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a distressed patient experiencing severe, continuous burning oral pain peaking late at night, leading to panic, insomnia, and refusal to swallow saliva. Urgent assessment was required to definitively exclude acute caustic injury, severe oral candidiasis, or anaphylaxis, and to provide immediate palliative analgesia and reassurance. This acute symptomatic distress and dehydration risk necessitated intervention before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My tongue feels like I drank boiling water, I can't sleep, and there's this awful battery taste in my mouth constantly."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a continuous, severe scalding sensation strictly affecting the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. The pain progressively worsens throughout the day, peaking now in the late evening, preventing sleep. The patient notes a persistent metallic taste (dysgeusia) and a subjective sensation of intense dry mouth, though they note the burning completely stops temporarily while actively chewing food or gum. Symptoms have caused severe acute anxiety.
Functional Status: Independent but acutely distressed and sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; escalating psychosocial stress from recent bereavement compounding symptom severity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, exhausted, frequently sipping water but finding no relief.
Physical Exam: Oral examination reveals a completely normal-appearing oral mucosa. No localized erythema, no ulceration, and complete absence of candidal plaques. Bilateral and highly symmetrical symptom distribution on the anterior tongue. Objective salivary flow appears normal (saliva pooling under the tongue). No cervical lymphadenopathy. Cranial nerves V and VII intact.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty bottles of caustic mouthwashes in the bathroom, likely exacerbating the mucosal sensitivity.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Burning Mouth Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Oral Candidiasis - Ruled out due to completely normal, pink mucosa with no white plaques or removable pseudomembranes.

Sjogren's Syndrome - Ruled out due to objective presence of saliva pooling in the floor of the mouth and absence of xerophthalmia (dry eyes).

Contact Stomatitis - Ruled out due to absence of mucosal erythema, swelling, or sloughing despite the severe burning sensation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to severe, intractable burning pain in the oral cavity that climaxed in the late evening, causing acute psychological distress and complete insomnia. The severity of the subjective pain necessitated an immediate physical examination to rule out acute infectious processes, chemical burns, or systemic autoimmune flares. Confirming the visual normality of the mucosa was vital to safely defer invasive investigations, prevent an inappropriate midnight presentation to the emergency department, and immediately prescribe topical palliative treatments. The inability of the patient to cope with the pain at home justifies the urgency of this MBS Item 597 encounter.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Clonazepam (sucking and spitting a 0.5mg tablet) for localized neuropathic relief. Advised to cease all alcohol-based mouthwashes immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change: Replace caustic mouthwashes with frequent sips of ice water or chewing sugar-free gum. The immediate mechanical relief of pain during chewing will positively reinforce the transition away from harsh chemical rinses that aggravate the sensory nerves.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week for fasting glucose, B12, and iron studies to exclude secondary organic causes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing airway swelling, sudden difficulty breathing, inability to swallow liquids, or sudden facial droop.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and presumptive; blood tests are required to definitively exclude nutritional deficiencies or autoimmune conditions.
Clinical Course: Burning Mouth Syndrome is a benign but highly frustrating neuropathic condition. Symptoms typically peak in the evening but can be managed effectively with topical medications and avoidance of irritants.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you experience any swelling of your lips or tongue, or if you have any trouble breathing or swallowing water.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The nerves in your tongue are misfiring, sending a 'burn' signal without actual tissue damage. Sucking the Clonazepam tablet acts locally to calm these specific hyperactive nerves.
Actionable Steps: Chew sugar-free gum when the pain peaks. The act of chewing overrides the nerve signals causing the burning. Experiencing this relief will help you rely on mechanical soothing rather than over-washing your mouth.

